From 14d76098fb3687228d8e9cc7464b85674e158d90 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Austin Schuh <austin.linux@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:56:48 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] io_uring: don't trust an earlier task_work add to wake on
 PREEMPT_RT

io_req_local_work_add() skips the wakeup when the request it queued
behind was enough to satisfy the waiter, on the grounds that whoever
added that one has woken, or will wake, the waiting task.  The add is a
cmpxchg on work_llist followed some instructions later by
wake_up_state(), with no lock held.  On PREEMPT_RT the earlier adder can
be preempted between the two for as long as higher-priority work runs on
its CPU, and the waiter, whatever its priority, sleeps until then.
Nothing boosts the preempted adder, since it holds no lock.

This bites IORING_OP_MSG_RING into a DEFER_TASKRUN ring in particular:
the posts come from other tasks, of any priority, and a real-time
sender's post is skipped whenever a lower-priority sender's is pending
ahead of it.

On PREEMPT_RT, wake the waiter regardless.  When the waiter is already
awake the extra wake_up_state() finds it running and returns.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GpUwwinTvks4P7qHQAefc5

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 io_uring/io_uring.c | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/io_uring/io_uring.c b/io_uring/io_uring.c
index a938ea85f1fb..f8c953bd15c1 100644
--- a/io_uring/io_uring.c
+++ b/io_uring/io_uring.c
@@ -1320,8 +1320,16 @@ static void io_req_local_work_add(struct io_kiocb *req, unsigned flags)
 	/* not enough or no one is waiting */
 	if (nr_tw < nr_wait)
 		return;
-	/* the previous add has already woken it up */
-	if (nr_tw_prev >= nr_wait)
+	/*
+	 * The previous add has already woken it up, or is about to.  On
+	 * PREEMPT_RT "about to" can be a long time: the previous adder can be
+	 * preempted between its cmpxchg above and its wake_up_state(), for as
+	 * long as anything of higher priority runs on its CPU, and a waiter of
+	 * any priority sleeps until it gets to run again.  Nothing boosts it,
+	 * since no lock is held.  Wake the waiter ourselves there; a waiter
+	 * that is already awake costs a try_to_wake_up() that finds it running.
+	 */
+	if (!IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT) && nr_tw_prev >= nr_wait)
 		return;
 	wake_up_state(ctx->submitter_task, TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE);
 }
